1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,395
A website can work with a mouse and still fail during keyboard navigation.

2
00:00:04,395 --> 00:00:08,790
TabProof helps developers turn a keyboard failure into a reproducible test, then check

3
00:00:08,790 --> 00:00:10,480
whether a fix actually works.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,308
Here is DispatchDesk, our controlled test application. The task is to find Northstar

5
00:00:17,308 --> 00:00:22,615
Studio's order in transit, review a proposed delivery date, and close the preview

6
00:00:22,615 --> 00:00:23,840
without applying changes.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,182
Meta Muse Spark interprets screenshots, page semantics, focus, and action history to choose

8
00:00:30,182 --> 00:00:35,364
the next step. We supply the business goal, not a navigation script. The

9
00:00:35,364 --> 00:00:39,350
agent's only action tools are keyboard presses and text entry.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,823
Watch this recorded route from the third pair. The agent passes a similarly

11
00:00:44,823 --> 00:00:49,646
named customer, opens the correct order, and selects the requested date. It briefly

12
00:00:49,646 --> 00:00:54,469
overshoots a control, then recovers. Eighteen real keyboard actions reach the preview. These

13
00:00:54,469 --> 00:00:58,550
are observations from the saved Meta session, not a hand-authored route.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,561
Now the first Escape leaves the preview open, with focus still inside it.

15
00:01:09,561 --> 00:01:14,123
TabProof records the observed dialog state independently of what the model says. This

16
00:01:14,123 --> 00:01:16,930
is the specific keyboard behavior we are measuring.

17
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,908
The agent eventually finds a way out after more actions and reports completion.

18
00:01:24,908 --> 00:01:29,817
That does not erase the earlier Escape failures. TabProof keeps eventual recovery and

19
00:01:29,817 --> 00:01:32,460
the measured interaction defect as separate results.

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,294
The two demo releases differ by one source change: removing the cancel handler

21
00:01:38,294 --> 00:01:42,587
that prevents default dialog dismissal. The generated pages are checked for this exact

22
00:01:42,587 --> 00:01:46,220
difference. This is a seeded bug in our own test application.

23
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,673
The independent checker replays the original agent's exact route on both versions, in

24
00:01:52,673 --> 00:01:57,346
three fresh browsers each. It checks focus along the way, then tests one

25
00:01:57,346 --> 00:02:01,300
Escape. A changed route becomes inconclusive instead of a guessed defect.

26
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,468
A fresh Meta attempt also reaches the corrected preview. Its first Escape closes

27
00:02:07,468 --> 00:02:11,936
the dialog and returns focus to the opener. The independently replayed original route

28
00:02:11,936 --> 00:02:16,060
passes too. The model outcome and the keyboard measurements are both retained.

29
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,117
We ran three new pairs, six actual Meta sessions, with no API failures.

30
00:02:23,117 --> 00:02:28,233
All six reached the target. Every original first Escape failed; every corrected first

31
00:02:28,233 --> 00:02:33,350
Escape passed. The eighteen independent reproductions are separate checks, not extra model runs.

32
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,567
Developers can start and stop runs, inspect actions and screenshots, compare versions, and

33
00:02:38,567 --> 00:02:43,134
download an offline evidence report. Instead of manually writing the initial path, they

34
00:02:43,134 --> 00:02:47,350
can inspect the path discovered by Meta and reproduce the measured interaction.

35
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,760
That is TabProof: Meta discovers a path, and independent checks measure the fix.

36
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:57,519
This small controlled study is not an accessibility certification. Our code, raw results,

37
00:02:57,519 --> 00:02:59,350
and reproduction steps are public.
